proposal.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "lifted_count",
      "value_type": "count",
      "description": "When several highlighted cubes had to be picked up, the prescribed red-first, increasing-world-y order contradicted the recorded pickups. A highlighted blue cube was lifted and returned before green and red were handled, and among two highlighted blue cubes the larger-world-y cube was handled first. Consequently the prefix count could remain zero after a real completed pickup, making later target selection fail SUFFICIENCY. Replace the fixed order with blue, green, red, omitting unhighlighted colors; within each color use decreasing original world y, then increasing original world x for ties. Establish identities and this order from highlight_frame, never from positions changed by handling. Keep the field as the number of completed lifts in this fixed prefix. Initialize to 0 and increment only at the cycle observing the next target held off the table with TCP approximately z=0.15. Do not increment at grasp or when commanding the lift, and do not decrement after returning a cube. For example, after the first highlighted blue cube has visibly reached lift height, write 1 and preserve 1 through its release and retreat. Reannotate subsequent counts using this corrected order; no additional memory field is needed."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "lifted_count",
      "description": "With a highlighted blue cube already lifted and returned, the old red-first order left the completed-prefix count at zero and made the policy select red instead of the next green target. The same conflict occurred between two highlighted blue cubes ordered by increasing world y. This breaks SUFFICIENCY and would break PREDICTABILITY if the annotation were corrected without changing the policy's definition. Replace the order with blue, green, red, omitting unhighlighted colors; within each color use decreasing original world y, then increasing original world x for ties. Recover identities and their original order from highlight_frame. Initialize lifted_count to 0. Using the carried count, identify the next uncounted target; increment by one only when this cycle's motion frames and current state show that target newly lifted and held off the table at TCP approximately z=0.15. For example, write 1 after the first blue target's completed lift and retain it through release and retreat. Do not increment for a low grasp, a commanded lift, an empty grasp, a previously counted cube still held, or an action executing no steps. Preserve all other existing completion checks."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "After the button operation, the old target-order rule selected red instead of the recorded blue target, or the smaller-world-y blue cube instead of the larger-world-y one. After a completed lift and return, the inconsistent count again selected the wrong target. These are SUFFICIENCY failures, not reasons for cycle-specific exceptions. Replace every red-green-blue and increasing-world-y ordering clause with the same fixed order used by memory: blue, green, red, omitting unhighlighted colors; within each color use decreasing original world y, then increasing original world x for ties. Match individual highlighted identities from highlight_frame and do not reorder after handling. Recognize a newly completed lift from this cycle's observations before choosing the next action. Retain the existing return-and-retreat rules: a counted cube still held is returned only if another target remains, a low open hand retracts before lateral travel, and an open hand already hovering over the selected uncounted cube descends and closes rather than switching targets or repeating the hover. Remove the conflicting old ordering clauses entirely.\n\nA separate open-hand approach to the correct highlighted cube was displaced by more than 1 cm and used a reflected yaw despite available calibration. This is a geometry-to-waypoint SUFFICIENCY failure, not missing historical information. Make the existing calibrated localization rule operational: for image pixel p=(u,v,1), form the world ray origin C=-R^T t and direction d=R^T K^-1 p. Intersect the ray with the relevant surface plane z=h using X=C+((h-C_z)/d_z)d. For resting cubes use the top-face height inferred from cube geometry, not the tabletop plane or the projected center of the entire visible silhouette. Localize the top-face center, refine with the current wrist view, and use its world x,y with the prescribed TCP approach height z=0.15. Reconstruct two endpoints of a top-face edge on that same plane, compute theta=atan2(delta_y,delta_x), and choose among theta+k*pi/2 the edge-aligned yaw nearest the current hand yaw. Do not negate theta based on image appearance or copy an image-plane edge angle. Keep that alignment for descent and lift. Retain the existing approximately 1–1.5 cm hover tolerance and object-centered descent rule.",
  "refused": "Some reports described the first handled target as a red cube and proposed remembering whether red had already been lifted. The full trajectories show that the first handled cube was blue, followed by green and then red. The report calling the blue cube beneath the hand a highlighted rear red cube is therefore not passed on, nor is a red-specific completion field or selection exception. The corrected common ordering and existing lifted_count represent the actual history. Likewise, the proposal to choose green despite an unchanged zero count is not retained as an action exception: the count must become 1 when the preceding blue lift is observed. Requests to descend when already within hover tolerance and to preserve highlighted identities despite occlusion are already covered by the action instructions; they require consistent target selection, not additional memory or separate special-case rules."
}

optimizer.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": [
    {
      "modify_from": "Back-project a visible surface center onto that surface's horizontal height plane, not automatically onto z=0. Estimate surface height and center from object geometry and both calibrated current views where available.",
      "modify_to": "Back-project a visible surface center onto that surface's horizontal height plane, not automatically onto z=0. Estimate surface height and center from object geometry and both calibrated current views where available. For pixel p=(u,v,1), use the corresponding camera's calibration to compute world ray origin C=-R^T t and direction d=R^T K^-1 p. Intersect with the surface plane z=h using X=C+((h-C_z)/d_z)d. Use the current wrist camera's extrinsics for wrist pixels, not the front camera's extrinsics."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "For a resting cube, localize the top-face center at the cube's top height.",
      "modify_to": "For a resting cube, localize the top-face center at the cube's top height inferred from its geometry, not the tabletop plane or the center of the entire visible silhouette. Refine this estimate with the current calibrated wrist view."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Reconstruct the cube's top-face edge directions in the horizontal world plane using camera calibration and the top-face height; do not copy image edge angles as world yaw. Among the square-equivalent edge-aligned yaws, choose the one requiring the least rotation from the current hand yaw. Preserve that alignment during descent and lift.",
      "modify_to": "Back-project two endpoints of a cube top-face edge onto the same top-face height plane using the calibrated ray-plane intersection. From their world-coordinate difference compute theta=atan2(delta_y,delta_x). Choose among theta+k*pi/2, for integer k, the square-equivalent edge-aligned yaw requiring the least angular rotation from the current hand yaw, accounting for angle wrapping. Do not negate theta based on image appearance or copy an image-plane edge angle. Preserve that alignment during descent and lift."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "Do not replace the shared target order with blue-green-red and decreasing original world y, or reannotate prefix counts on that basis. Round 1 established robot-right-to-left same-color handling; Round 2 proposed green-first handling; this round proposes blue-first handling. No reported observable condition explains when each order applies. The latest proposal would replace one unsupported universal ordering with another and undo the earlier same-color correction. Retain the existing ordering pending evidence of a selection rule that explains the conflicting trajectories; the ordering failures remain unresolved. Do not add color-specific completion fields, relabel the observed blue cube as red, or introduce an exception selecting green with an unchanged count. The existing aligned-target descent, identity matching, completion checks, and return-and-retreat rules need no duplicate clauses. The committed geometry edits operationalize the existing calibration rules without reversing earlier edits."
}
